<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">La Venta</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.) · capital La Venta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Olmec centre after San Lorenzo, an island capital in the Tabasco swamps famous for its clay pyramid, colossal heads and buried jade offerings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>La Venta rose on a small island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonala-river">Tonalá</a> river marshes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a> from about 900 BCE, as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/san-lorenzo">San Lorenzo</a> declined. Access to estuaries, fishing grounds and river routes supported a growing population and an elite that drew labour and goods from a wide area.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its ceremonial core, Complex A and the great clay pyramid, was laid out on a north-south axis. Rulers commissioned four colossal heads, altars showing figures emerging from caves, and stelae. Massive offerings of serpentine blocks, jade celts and mosaic pavements were buried beneath plazas, showing enormous investment in ritual. Jade and other prestige goods came from distant sources, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 400 BCE La Venta was abandoned and many monuments were damaged. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec</a> heartland lost its leading role, but La Venta's art, ritual and ideas of kingship passed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epi-olmec-culture">Epi-Olmec</a>, Maya and other later traditions.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/la-venta.jpg" alt="La Venta" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Alfonsobouchot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Venta_Pir%C3%A1mide_cara_norte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 400 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>La Venta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Olmec religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Probably Mixe-Zoquean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Olmec La Venta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-650&amp;focus=la-venta" aria-label="La Venta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2130 1506 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where La Venta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2450 1762L2427 1776"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2427 1776h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2450 1762h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2450 1762h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-650&amp;focus=la-venta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
